.TH "vsearch-makeudb_usearch" "1" "September 18, 2026" "version 2.32.0" "vsearch manual"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
vsearch --makeudb_usearch \[em] create a UDB database file from a fasta
or fastq file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\f[B]vsearch\f[R] \f[B]--makeudb_usearch\f[R] \f[I]fastxfile\f[R]
\f[B]--output\f[R] \f[I]dbfile\f[R] [\f[I]options\f[R]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
The vsearch command \f[V]--makeudb_usearch\f[R] creates a UDB database
file from the sequences in \f[I]fastxfile\f[R], in fasta or fastq format
(quality values are ignored and are not stored in the database).
The UDB file is a binary format that contains the sequences together
with a \f[I]k\f[R]-mer index, and can be quickly loaded into memory.
Using a UDB file avoids re-indexing the database on every run, which is
worthwhile when the same database is searched repeatedly with
\f[V]--usearch_global\f[R] or \f[V]--sintax\f[R].
.PP
Both \f[V]--makeudb_usearch\f[R] and \f[V]--output\f[R] must be
specified.
.PP
The database must contain at least one sequence.
vsearch reports a fatal error and stops if \f[I]fastxfile\f[R] is empty,
or if all of its sequences are discarded by \f[V]--minseqlength\f[R] (32
nucleotides by default for this command), since a UDB file recording no
sequence cannot be read back.
.PP
The UDB file holds one 32-bit counter per possible \f[I]k\f[R]-mer,
whether or not the database contains that \f[I]k\f[R]-mer.
Those 4\[ha]\f[I]wordlength\f[R] counters are 256 kB at the default word
length of 8, but 1 GB at word length 14 and 4 GB at 15, whatever the
size of \f[I]fastxfile\f[R], so raising \f[V]--wordlength\f[R] grows the
output file as well as the memory needed to build it (see
\f[V]--wordlength\f[R]).
.PP
Of the work this command performs, only DUST masking is distributed over
several threads.
Reading the input, building the \f[I]k\f[R]-mer index and writing the
UDB file are single-threaded, and so is the letter replacement
\f[V]--hardmask\f[R] asks for.
\f[V]--threads\f[R] therefore shortens the masking step and nothing
else: it helps with the default \f[V]--dbmask dust\f[R], and has no
measurable effect with \f[V]--dbmask none\f[R] or
\f[V]--dbmask soft\f[R].
What it can save is bounded by the share of the run spent masking, which
grows with the length of the sequences, and on short ones the gain stops
increasing beyond a handful of threads.
.PP
See \f[V]vsearch-udb(5)\f[R] for a description of the UDB file format.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS mandatory options
.TP
\f[V]--makeudb_usearch\f[R] \f[I]fastxfile\f[R]
Read fasta or fastq sequences from \f[I]fastxfile\f[R] and create a UDB
database (quality values are ignored).
.TP
\f[V]--output\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write the UDB database to \f[I]filename\f[R].
.SS core options
.TP
\f[V]--dbmask\f[R] \f[I]none\f[R]|\f[I]dust\f[R]|\f[I]soft\f[R]
Mask regions in the database sequences using the \f[I]dust\f[R] method
or the \f[I]soft\f[R] method, or \f[I]none\f[R] to suppress masking.
See \f[V]vsearch-fastx_mask(1)\f[R] for more details.
Warning, when using \f[I]soft\f[R] masking, search commands become case
sensitive: masking excludes masked regions from the \f[I]k\f[R]-mer
index used to select candidate targets (the pairwise alignment itself
always ignores case).
A database sequence with no unmasked stretch of at least the word length
contributes no \f[I]k\f[R]-mer to the index and is never selected as a
candidate target; it can still be reported when the query itself samples
no \f[I]k\f[R]-mer, as such a query is compared against every database
sequence (see \f[V]--qmask\f[R]).
vsearch warns when there are such sequences, giving how many.
The default is to mask using \f[I]dust\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--hardmask\f[R]
Replace masked nucleotides with Ns, rather than lowercasing them.
.RS
.PP
This is also the only masking option that reaches the alignment.
Soft and dust masking (see \f[V]--qmask\f[R] and \f[V]--dbmask\f[R])
only keep masked words out of the \f[I]k\f[R]-mer pre-filter that
selects candidates; the masked region is still aligned and scored like
any other.
An N, on the other hand, scores zero and counts as a matching column, so
hard masking makes a masked region match whatever it is aligned against,
and the reported identity can only go up.
Add \f[V]--n_mismatch\f[R] to count those columns as mismatches instead.
.RE
.TP
\f[V]--wordlength\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the length of words (i.e.\ \f[I]k\f[R]-mers) used for sequence
indexing and comparisons.
Valid values range from 3 to 15.
The default is 8.
Note that the default \f[V]--minwordmatches\f[R] is derived from this
value, so changing one changes both (see \f[V]--minwordmatches\f[R]).
.RS
.PP
Longer words make the \f[I]k\f[R]-mer index more selective, so fewer
targets are offered as candidates and the search itself gets faster:
from word length 5 to 11 the search phase shrank by a factor of 2.4 on
130-nucleotide amplicons and 5.8 on full-length reference sequences.
Working against that, the index has 4\[ha]\f[I]wordlength\f[R] slots, so
the memory it needs and the time spent building it both quadruple with
each added nucleotide.
On a 400 000-sequence database the whole run needed 0.3 GB at word
length 10, 1.3 GB at 12 and 16 GB at 15.
.PP
The best setting balances the two, and depends on how many queries are
searched against a given database: with few queries the index build
dominates and a shorter word is cheaper overall, while with many queries
the search dominates and a longer word repays its index.
Changing the word length is not output-neutral, so it should be chosen
for a workload rather than tuned per run.
.RE
.SS secondary options
.TP
\f[V]--bzip2_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Huffman coding.
See \f[V]bzip2(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with bzip2,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--gzip_decompress\f[R]
Specify that the \f[I]input pipe\f[R] is streaming data compressed using
Lempel-Ziv coding.
See \f[V]gzip(1)\f[R] for more details.
This option is required when compressed data arrives on standard input
through a pipe (`-'), where the format cannot be detected without
consuming the stream.
It is not needed when reading from a regular file compressed with gzip,
nor when such a file is redirected to standard input: compression is
then detected automatically, and a contradicting option is ignored (with
a warning when the input is standard input).
Pipes other than standard input, such as shell process substitutions and
named FIFOs, are always read as uncompressed data; compressed data must
arrive on standard input or as a named file.
.TP
\f[V]--log\f[R] \f[I]filename\f[R]
Write messages to \f[I]filename\f[R].
Messages include program version, start and finish times, elapsed time,
amount of memory available, maximum amount of memory consumed, number of
cores and command line options, and if need be, command-specific
informational messages, warnings, and errors.
Messages are written \f[I]in addition\f[R] to the standard error, not
instead of it; use \f[V]--quiet\f[R] to silence the standard error copy
(see the \f[V]--quiet\f[R] entry for what it spares).
.TP
\f[V]--maxseqlength\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Discard sequences longer than \f[I]positive integer\f[R] (50,000
nucleotides by default).
The value must not exceed 2,147,481,646 (\f[V]INT_MAX\f[R] minus 2,001).
When that value is smaller than the effective \f[V]--minseqlength\f[R]
value (whose default is command-specific), no sequence can pass the
length filter: vsearch issues a warning.
.TP
\f[V]--minseqlength\f[R] \f[I]non-negative integer\f[R]
Discard sequences shorter than \f[I]non-negative integer\f[R] (32
nucleotides by default).
A value of 0 retains empty sequences.
When that value is larger than the \f[V]--maxseqlength\f[R] value, no
sequence can pass the length filter: vsearch issues a warning.
.TP
\f[V]--no_progress\f[R]
Suppress the gradually increasing progress indicator normally written to
the \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R].
.TP
\f[V]--notrunclabels\f[R]
Retain whole sequence headers in output files.
By default, vsearch truncates sequence headers at first space or
tabulation.
This option suppresses truncation.
.TP
\f[V]--quiet\f[R]
Suppress messages to the \f[I]standard output\f[R] \f[V]stdout(3)\f[R]
and \f[I]standard error\f[R] \f[V]stderr(3)\f[R], except for warnings
and error messages.
.TP
\f[V]--threads\f[R] \f[I]positive integer\f[R]
Set the number of computation threads to use, from 1 to 1024.
The number of threads should not exceed the number of available CPU
cores.
The value 0 is also accepted and, like the default, uses all available
cores; decimal values are truncated to their integer part.
On Linux, \[lq]available\[rq] accounts for the CPU affinity mask and the
cgroup CPU quota of the running process, so a job confined by
\f[B]taskset\f[R](1), Slurm, Docker or Kubernetes launches one thread
per core it was actually granted, rather than one per core the machine
has.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Create a UDB database from a fasta file with default settings:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --makeudb_usearch db.fasta \[rs]
    --output db.udb
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Create a UDB database without masking:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --makeudb_usearch db.fasta \[rs]
    --dbmask none \[rs]
    --output db.udb
\f[R]
.fi
.PP
Use the resulting UDB file with \f[V]--usearch_global\f[R]:
.IP
.nf
\f[C]
vsearch \[rs]
    --makeudb_usearch db.fasta \[rs]
    --output db.udb

vsearch \[rs]
    --usearch_global queries.fasta \[rs]
    --db db.udb \[rs]
    --id 0.97 \[rs]
    --blast6out results.b6
\f[R]
.fi
